  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nPrecipitation will continue as a low pressure system traverses \nthe area today, then a passing shortwave will prolong \nprecipitation chances into the night while the main system \nretreats northeast into New England.\n\nRain will be the dominant precipitation type for the lowlands, \nthough some locations could see a few snowflakes early this morning. \nMeanwhile, rain is expected to transition to snow in the mountains, \nwith snow showers, or a combination of snow and freezing drizzle, \nanticipated to impact the higher terrain through early Saturday \nmorning. \n\nAt least a few tenths of an inch of snow are expected to accrue \nalong the mountains, with higher amounts -- on the order of two to \nfour inches -- possible within the high elevation zones of the \nnortheast mountains. Freezing drizzle could also deposit a light \nglaze of ice across parts of the northeast mountains early Saturday \nmorning. A Winter Weather Advisory has been issued for the high \nelevations of the northeast mountains where impacts from snow and/or \nfreezing drizzle are most likely to develop today through Saturday \nmorning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nGusty winds are expected to persist across the area, with 25 to 35 \nmph gusts in the lowlands and near advisory level gusts possible \nalong the mountains today. Blustery conditions are likely to linger \nthrough the night, particularly along the higher terrain, before \neasing up on Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nDrier conditions briefly return on Saturday, then a cold front \nbrings the next opportunity for precipitation Sunday afternoon \ninto Monday. Precipitation will initially fall as all rain, \nthen a transition to snow is expected to occur along the \nmountains before precipitation comes to an end.",
